The answer is  $\lceil n/3\rceil$  and as an example consider  $n=6$  and a splitting of a concave hexagon as follows.
[asy]
pair A = dir(-30);
pair B = dir(90);
pair C = dir(210);
pair D = (2*C + A) / 3;
pair E = dir(-90);
pair F = (C + 2*A) / 3;

filldraw(A--B--C--cycle, paleblue, blue);
filldraw(D--E--F--cycle, pink, red);

dot(A);
dot(B);
dot(C);
dot(D);
dot(E);
dot(F);
[/asy]
In this case, the minimum required number of triangles is clearly  $2$ . Generally, it's possible to construct a concave  $n$ -gon composed of  $\lceil n/3\rceil$  triangles.

Now, if  $t$  is the number of triangles in a splitting, we must prove that  $n\le 3t$ . But clearly, each vertex of the polygon corresponds to at least one vertex of a triangle, and each triangle has  $3$  vertices. This completes the proof.

