We wish to show  $$ \frac{x + 3}{y + z} + \frac{y + 3}{z + x} + \frac{z + 3}{x + y} + 3 \ge 27 \cdot \frac{(\sqrt{x} + \sqrt{y} + \sqrt{z})^2}{(x + y + z)^3}. $$  under the constraint  $x,y,z \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$  and  $xy+yz+zx=3$ .
====
\begin{align*}
\textsf{LHS} &= \sum_{\text{cyc}} \left( \frac{x+3}{y+z} + 1\right) 
&= \left( x+y+z+3 \right) \times \left( \sum_{\text{cyc}} \frac{1}{x+y} \right) 
&\geq \frac{9}{2} \times \left( x + y + z + 3 \right) \times \frac{1}{x+y+z} & \textsf{(Cauchy-Schwarz)}
\end{align*}
On the other hand, 
\begin{align*}
\textsf{RHS} &= 27 \times \frac{(\sqrt{x} + \sqrt{y} + \sqrt{z})^2}{(x+y+z)^3} 
&= 27 \times \frac{x+y+z+2\sqrt{xy}+2\sqrt{yz}+2\sqrt{zx}}{(x+y+z)^3} 
&\leq 27 \times \frac{x+y+z+2x+2y+2z}{(x+y+z)^3} && \textsf{(AM-GM)} 
&= 81 \times \frac{1}{(x+y+z)^2}
\end{align*}
Finally, let  $s = x + y + z$ . <details><summary>Note </summary>Note that
\begin{align*}
s^2 &= x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 2(xy+yz+zx) 
&= x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 6 && \textsf{(due to  $xy+yz+zx=3$ )} 
&\geq xy+yz+zx+ 6 && \textsf{(AM-GM)} 
&= 9 && \textsf{(due to  $xy+yz+zx=3$ )} 
\end{align*}</details>
It suffices to show that when  $s^2 \geq 9 \implies s \geq 3$ , 
\begin{align*}
\frac{9(s+3)}{2s} &\geq \frac{81}{s^2} 
\implies 9s^2 +27s &\geq 162
\end{align*}
which is evidently true as  $9s^2 + 27s \geq 9(3)^2+27(3) = 162$ . Equality holds when  $x+y+z=3$ .

