<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $a=x+y$  and  $b=xy$ , then we have
\begin{align*} x^3 + y^3 + (x + y)^3 + 30xy &= 2000 
(x+y)^3-3xy(x+y)+(x+y)^3+30xy&=2000 
a^3-3ab+a^3+30b&=2000 
2a^3-3ab+30b-2000&=0 
(a-10)(2a^2+10a-3b+200)&=0
\end{align*}
We get the factorization using synthetic division and the rational root theorem.  Then, we have the only two possible solutions that are
\begin{align*} a&=10 2a^2+10a-3b+200&=0\end{align*}
The first case is just  $a=x+y=10$  and we're done.

The second case is just a tiny bit trickier.  We use the quadratic formula to get \[a\&=\frac{-10\pm\sqrt{100-8(200-3b)}}{2} 
\&=-5\pm\sqrt{25-400+6b} 
\&=-5\pm\sqrt{6b-375}
\]
Since  $x$  and  $y$  are positive, we have  $a>0$  as well.  Thus, the only possibility is  $a=-5+\sqrt{6b-375}$  because  $-5-\sqrt{6b-375}<0$ .  Thus, we have
\begin{align*}a&>0 
-5+\sqrt{6b-375}&>0 
\sqrt{6b-375}&>5 
6b-375&>25 
6b&>400 
b&>\frac{200}{3} 
xy&>\frac{200}{3}
\end{align*}
However, we have  $x^3 + y^3 + (x + y)^3 + 30xy \&= 2000$  but  $30xy>30\left(\frac{200}{3}\right)=2000$  and every term is greater than  $0$  therefore we have the quadratic providing faulty solutions.

Thus, the only solution is  $a=x+y=10$  as desired. $\blacksquare$</details>

