\measuredangle AB + \measuredangle AC &= 0&&\text{because }\triangle BAC\text{ is isosceles}
\measuredangle AB-\measuredangle BD &= \measuredangle BE-\measuredangle BC&&\text{because }\angle ABD = \angle CBE
\measuredangle AC-\measuredangle BD&=\measuredangle BD-\measuredangle BE&&\text{because }\triangle DEB\text{ is isosceles}\end{align*}
Substituting the first two into the sum of the second two gives  $3\measuredangle BD = 0\pmod{180^\circ}$ , so  $\measuredangle BD=60^\circ$ .

We're asked to find  $\angle COD$ .  Mod  $180^\circ$ , that's equal to  $\measuredangle DO-\measuredangle CO$ .  We're given one angle bisector ( $2\measuredangle CO=\measuredangle AC+\measuredangle BC=\measuredangle AC$ ) and from the existence of the incentre we know another ( $2\measuredangle DO=\measuredangle BD+\measuredangle AC$ ), so subtracting we get  $2\angle COD=\measuredangle BD=60^\circ\pmod{180^\circ}$ , so  $\angle COD=120^\circ$ .</details>

