We proceed by complex numbers with unit circle as  $(ABC)$ . Suppose  $A=a^2, B=b^2,C=c^2$ . Therefore,  $i=-(ab+bc+ca)$  and  $s=-bc$ . Suppose  $Q$  is the second intersection of  $(ABC)$  and circle with center  $I$  and radius  $AI$ .

As  $QI=IA$  and  $Q$  and  $A$  lie on unit circle, \[(q-i)(\overline{q}-\overline{i})=(a^2-i)(\overline{a^2}-\overline{i})\implies (q-a^2)\overline{i}=\frac{i}{a^2}-\frac{i}{q}=\frac{i(q-a^2)}{qa^2}.\]Thus, \[q=\frac{i}{a^2\overline{i}}=\frac{(ab+bc+ca)abc}{a^2(a+b+c)}=\frac{(ab+bc+ca)bc}{a(a+b+c)}.\]
As  $J$  is the reflection of  $I$  over  $BC$ , \[j=\frac{-(b^2-c^2)\frac{a+b+c}{abc}+\frac{(c^2-b^2)(c^2+b^2)}{b^2c^2}}{\frac{c^2-b^2}{b^2c^2}}=\frac{(a+b+c)bc}{a}+b^2+c^2.\]Thus, \[\overline{j}=\frac{ab+bc+ca+b^2+c^2}{b^2c^2}.\]
We need to show that  $P\equiv Q$  or  $Q,J,S$  are collinear.

Therefore we need to show, \[\frac{q-s}{j-s}=\frac{\frac{1}{q}-\frac{1}{s}}{\overline{j}-\frac{1}{s}}=\frac{s-q}{q(s\overline{j}-1)}\Leftrightarrow q(1-s\overline{j})=j-s.\]
We have,
\begin{align*}
q(1-s\overline{j})&=\frac{(ab+bc+ca)bc}{a(a+b+c)}\left(1+bc\cdot \frac{ab+bc+ca+b^2+c^2}{b^2c^2}\right)
&= \frac{(ab+bc+ca)bc}{a(a+b+c)}\left(\frac{(b+c)^2+a(b+c)}{bc}\right)
&= \frac{(ab+bc+ca)(b+c)}{a}.
\end{align*}
Also,
\begin{align*}
j-s&=\frac{(a+b+c)bc}{a}+b^2+c^2+bc
&= \frac{bc(a+b+c)+a(b^2+c^2)+abc}{a}
&= \frac{a(b+c)^2+bc(b+c)}{a}
&= \frac{(b+c)(ab+bc+ca)}{a}.
\end{align*}
Hence,  $q(1-s\overline{j})=j-s$  therefore,  $J,Q,S$  are collinear giving  $Q\equiv P$ .

Proved.

