Ones that don't apply to this specific case of  $N$  include the purple fat stair case in which each stair has length  $2$  and one end has length  $1$ , and the two above red+blue+purple configurations that result when removing the two purple squares.

As such, the answer for general large  $N$  is  $5$  for odd  $N$  and  $3$  for even  $N$ .

Call an isometry  $f$  of the plane *lossy* of degree  $k$  for an integer  $k$  and a finite subset  $S \subset \mathbb{Z}^2$  if  $|S \setminus f(S)| = k$ . Then a caterpillar is any polyomino such that by applying a series of isometries lossy of degree  $1$  we can move the polyomino to be completely disjoint with itself.**<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** The degree of lossiness of the composition of two isometries  $f, g$  on the same  $S$  is at most the sum of their degrees  $k_f, k_g$ .
*Proof.* Note that  $|S \setminus f(g(S))| \le |S \setminus f(S)| + |f(S) \setminus f(g(S))|$  as if  $s \in S, s \not\in f(g(S))$  then  $s$  is in at least one of the two sets.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>**  $f$  is lossy with degree  $k$  iff  $f^{-1}$  is. 
*Proof.* Consider chains formed by  $f$ .  $\blacksquare$ Now, our isometries used on the catepillar must be reflections about a line or point, or a  $90^\circ$  rotation.

Call an isometry *translatory* if it is a translation or a reflection plus translation along the slope of the reflection.**<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** If all isometries of  $P$  share a center (or fix a center) then we get a contradiction.
*Proof.* Note that the group spanned by isometries is a subgroup of  $D_8$  so has size at most  $24$ , giving a contradiction.  $\blacksquare$ Take our lossy isometries to have reference wrt to the polymino and its orientation and reflection. We now show that if we have too many lossy isometries then this forces some translatory isometries which forces a contradiction.**<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** If  $P$  has a lossy translatory isometry of degree  $k$  then  $P$  fits in  $2k$  consecutive rows / columns / diagonals, where  $2k$  is specifically for when there's a reflection.
*Proof.* Translatory just means that our chains aren't cycle and our chains lie within  $1$  or  $2$  rows / columns / diagonals. Then the connected condition on the polymino means that the result follows.**<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** There's no rotations.
*Proof.* If there's two rotations  $r_1, r_2$ , then by composing them twice we get  $r_1 \circ r_1 \circ r_2 \circ r_2$  is a lossy translatory isometry of degree  $8$  by some fixed vector of the form  $(\pm 1, \pm 1)$ , so all of the polymino lies in  $8$  diagonals. However, then this evidently isn't fixed under rotations.

If there's a rotation  $r$  and a reflection about a point  $p$  without the same center, then  $r \circ r \circ p$  gives a contradiction similarly. Similarly, for reflection about a line  $l$  not through the rotation center,  $l \circ r \circ r \circ l$  is translatory isometry as well.

Else, all the rotations / reflections share a center giving a contradiction.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** If there are two distinct reflections of the same slope then there's a translatory isometry with lossy degree  $2$  or two distinct reflections about points.
*Proof.* Obvious.  $\blacksquare$ Thus, this implies that our reflections lie within at most  $2$  adjacent lines, which forces the equality cases of the  $2 \times (N+1)$  without corner and stair case.**<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** If there are three distinct reflections of different slopes then there's a translatory isometry with degree  $4$ .
*Proof.* Let  $f, g, h$  be the reflections where the directed angles between  $f, g$  and  $g, h$  are both  $45^\circ$ . Then consider  $f \circ g \circ h \circ g$ .

This implies that our polymino fits within  $8$  columns / rows / diagonals. However since  $N$  is large and there are three reflections of different slopes then one reflection obviously can't be lossy.

If our isometries share a fixed point we are done b above. Else, we have the following case of an isometry one reflection about a line and one about a point not on the line.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** If there is a reflection about a line and a reflection about a point not on the line then there's a translatory isometry with degree  $2$ . 
*Proof.* Compose the two of them.  $\blacksquare$ This implies that the polymino fits within at most  $4$  columns / rows (WLOG rows) / diagonals, and furthermore, if we let the translatory reflective isometry be  $p$ , then it consists of at most two chains on  $p$ . If all elements lie in one chain then it reduces to a stair case configuration. Else, we can see the translation part must be  $(\pm 1, \pm 1)$  or  $(\pm 1, 0)$  else the chains can't be connected. Then there exists a middle chain (displayed in red in the original diagrams). Fix this middle chain, then the first column / diagonal must have one element on one side and two elements on the other side to empty a column / diagonal, which forces the caterpillars given originally.

This covers all cases of isometries so we are done.

---------------------------------------------**<span style="color:pink">Remark:</span>** We can also consider the centroid of  $P$ . Note that if the center is  $(x, y) \pmod{1}$ , then after isometries on  $\mathbb{Z}^2$ , the center is still  $(\pm x, \pm y)$  or  $(\pm y, \pm x) \pmod{1}$ .
Furthermore, if the centroid is originally  $d$  and a lossy isometry is applied, the taxicab distance between the new point is at most  $1$  (and equality case holds if the taxicab distance between the two differing squares is  $N$ ).

Thus, if we let our center of our original  $P$  be green, we have the following possible operations in general (Technical: Our diagram includes two pairs of points which can't both be within  $1$  of our point. We also consider colored points instead of points, which allows for our points to overlap without any consequence.)

We consider the points within  $1$  of our green point, with the boundary of the blue square. We color the points on the boundary blue, and the remaining points purple and black where black points indicate polymino configs which aren't reflected compare to  $P$ .

[asy]
real x = 0.2;
real y = 0.31;

real s = 2 - x;
real t = 2 - y;


void dotd(real a, real b) {
	pen p = gray;

	if (a == s && b == t) {
    	p = green;
    } else {
    	real k = abs(a - s) + abs(b - t);
        if (k >= 0.99 && k <= 1.01) {
            p = blue;
        } else if (k < 1.2) {
        	real fa = (a - floor(a)) - 0.5;
            real fb = (b - floor(b)) - 0.5;
        	if ((abs(fa) > abs(fb)) ^ (fa > 0) ^ (fb > 0)) {
        		p = black;
            } else {
            	p = purple;
            }
      	}
    }

	dot((a, b), p);
}

void drawd(real a, real b) {
	dotd(a+x, b+y);
    dotd(a+1-x, b+y);
	dotd(a+x, b+1-y);
	dotd(a+1-x, b+1-y);
	dotd(a+y, b+x);
	dotd(a+1-y, b+x);
	dotd(a+y, b+1-x);
	dotd(a+1-y, b+1-x);
}

int n = 3;
for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {
	draw((0,i)--(3,i), dotted);
    draw((i,0)--(i,3), dotted);
    if (i == n) {
    	break;
    }
    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    	drawd(i,j);
    }
}
