Here is <details><summary>my solution</summary>For a circle  $\Gamma$  and a point  $X$  let  $\rho(X,\Gamma)$  be the power of the point  $X$  respect to the circle  $\Gamma$ .
Let  $H$  and  $O$  be the orthocenter and the circumcenter of the triangle  $ABC$ , respectively.**Claim.**  $H$  lies on the radical axis of the circles  $(AOQ)$  and  $(BOP)$ .
*Proof.* Let  $HP\cap (BOP)\setminus \{P\}=\{M\}$ ,  $HQ\cap (AOQ) \setminus\{Q\} =\{N\}$ . Let  $A'$  and  $B'$  be the antipodes of the points  $A$  and  $B$ , respectively, in  $(ABC)$ . Because  $\angle HPA=\angle HC_1A=90^\circ=\angle A'PA$  it follows that  $A'\in PH$ . Similarly  $B'\in QH$ .
Because
\[\angle HMO=\angle PMO=\angle PBO=\angle PBB'=\angle PA'B'=\angle
  HA'B',\]
it follows that  $MO\parallel A'B'$ . Similarly  $NO\parallel A'B'$ . Hence  $MN\parallel A'B'$ . By Thales's theorem in  $\triangle HA'B'$  it follows that  $\dfrac{HM}{HA'}=\dfrac{HN}{HB'}$ , which implies
\begin{align*}
\rho (H,(AOQ))&= HQ\cdot HN=\dfrac{HN}{HB'}\cdot (HQ\cdot HB')=
\dfrac{HN}{HB'}\cdot \rho(H,(ABC))
&=\dfrac{HM}{HA'}\cdot (HP\cdot
HA')=HP\cdot HM=\rho(H,(BOP)),
\end{align*}
which concludes the proof of the claim.  $\blacksquare$ [asy]
