Now, we have two cases**1.**  $AQ\nparallel BP$ . Let  $\{K\}=AQ\cap BP$ . Let  $\Psi$  be the inversion respect to  $(ABC)$ .
Note that for each  $X\in (ABC)$ :  $\Psi(X)=X$ . Hence  $\Psi(AQ)=(AOQ)$  \text{ and }  $\Psi(BP)=(BOP)$ ,  which implies  $K^*=\Psi(K)\in (AOQ) \cap (BOP)\setminus\{O\}$ .
From this and the claim it follows that  $H\in OK^*$ . Because  $K\in OK^*$  it follows that  $O$ ,  $H$ ,  $K^*$  and  $K$  are collinear, which implies that lines  $AQ$ ,  $BP$  and the Euler line  $HO$  of triangle  $ABC$  are concurrent.**2.**  $AQ\parallel BP$ . In this case  $AQBP$  is an isosceles trapezium and the circumcenters of  $\triangle AOQ$  and  $\triangle BOP$ lies on the (common) perpendicular bisector of the segments  $AQ$  and  $BP$ , which implies that  $(AOQ)$  and  $(BOP)$  are tangent at  $O$ . From the claim it follows that  $HO$  is tangent to  $(BOP)$  at  $O$ , which implies
\[\angle HOP=\angle OBP=\angle OPB.\]
Hence  $HO\parallel BP\parallel AQ$ , and we are done.</details> submitted in the contest.

Here is <details><summary>my solution</summary>Because the four lines are not concurrent and no three of which are parallel, if no three of which are concurrent it follows that these lines are the sides of a quadrilateral  $O'X'Y'Z'$  (which can be concave). Using the Fundamental Theorem of Affine Transformations it follows that there exists an affine transformation  $f$  such that  $O(0,0)$ ,  $X(1,0)$  and  $Y(0,1)$  when  $O=f(O'),$   $X=f(X')$ ,  $Y=f(Y')$ . 
Let  $Z(a,b)=f(Z')$ . Note that  $a,b\ne 0$ .
Notice that the inverse  $f^{-1}$  of the (bijective) function  $f$  is also an affine transformation. Therefore if we found four points  $M$ ,  $N$ ,  $P$ ,  $Q$  on the sides of  $OXYZ$  which are collinear in this order such that  $MN=NP=PQ$ , which implies that the points  $A=f^{-1}(M)$ ,   $B=f^{-1}(N)$ ,  $C=f^{-1}(P)$  and  $D=f^{-1}(Q)$  satisfy the properties  $(i)$ ,  $(ii)$  and  $(iii)$ , and the problem si solved.
Now, we construct the points  $M$ ,  $N$ ,  $P$  and  $Q$  using analytic geometry. We denote the position vector of a point  $V$  by  $\vec{V}$  and  $m_\ell$  be the slope of the line  $\ell$ .
We have three cases:**1.**  $1\in \{a, b\}$ . If  $a=1$  and  $b=1$ ,  $OXYZ$  is a square and we can consider  $N$  and  $P$  as midpoints of  $[OY]$  and  $[YZ]$  and  $M\in NP\cap OX$ ,  $Q\in NP\cap ZX$ . If  $a=1$  and  $b\ne 1$ , let  $m=-\dfrac12$ ,  $n=\dfrac{b+1}4$  and  $M(m,0)$ ,  $N(0,n)$ ,  $P(-m, 2n)$ ,  $Q(-2m,3n)$ . Is trivial that  $\dfrac12(\vec{M}+\vec{P})=\vec{N},\;\dfrac12(\vec{N}+ \vec{Q})=\vec{P}\text{ and } \dfrac12(\vec{M}+\overrightarrow{Q})=\dfrac12(\vec{N}+\vec{P})$  which implies   $M$ ,  $N$ ,  $P$ ,  $Q$  are collinear in this order such that  $MN=NP=PQ$ . Because  $Q(1,3n)\in ZX$  and  $P\in YZ$  because  $m_{ZY}=m_{PY}\iff \dfrac{b-1}1=\dfrac{2n-1}{1/2}\iff n=\dfrac{b+1}4,$  true.
Similarly we analize the case  $b=1$  and  $a\ne 1$  using the  points  $M(m,0)$ ,  $N(0,n)$ ,  $P(-m, 2n)$ ,  $Q(-2m,3n)$  with  $n=\dfrac12$  and  $m=\dfrac{1-3a}4$ , and by proving that  $m_{ZX}=m_{QX}$ \[\iff \dfrac1{a-1}=\dfrac{3n}{-2m-1}\iff
(-4m-2)=3a-3\iff 4m=1-3a,\text{ true}\]**2.**  $a, b\ne 1$  and  $ab+3a+3b-3\ne 0$ . Let   $n=\dfrac{2ab+b^2-b}{ab+3a+3b-3}$  and  $m=\dfrac{a(2n-1)}{1-b}$ .
First, we show that  $n=\dfrac{b(2m-1)}{3(1-a)}$    $(1)$ It is reduced to  $3an-3n=-2bm-b$ \begin{align*}
&\iff 3an+b-3n+\frac{2ba(2n-1)}{1-b}\iff
3an(1-b)+b(1-b)-3n(1-b)+2ab(2n-1)=0
&\iff 3an-3abn+b^2-b-3n+3nb+4abn-2ab=0
&\iff n(3a+ab+3b-3)=2ab+b^2-b, \text{ which is obvious.}
\end{align*}
Let  $M(m,0)$ ,  $N(0,n)$ ,  $P(-m, 2n)$  and  $Q(-2m,3n)$ . Is trivial that
\[\frac{\vec{M}+\vec{P}}2=\vec{N},\;\frac{\vec{N}+
\vec{Q}}2=\vec{P}\text{ and } \frac{\vec{M}+\vec{Q}}2
=\frac{\vec{N}+\vec{P}}2 \tag{2}\]
which implies   $M$ ,  $N$ ,  $P$ ,  $Q$  are collinear in this order such that  $MN=NP=PQ$ .
Remains to show that  $P\in YZ$  and  $Q\in ZX$ .
If  $m\notin \{0,1/2\}$ :
\[
P\in YZ\iff m_{YZ}=m_{PY}\iff \frac{b-1}{a}=\frac{2n-1}{-m}\iff
 m(1-b)=a(2n-1),\tag{ $\spadesuit$ }
\]
which is clear. Similarly
\[
Q\in ZX \iff m_{ZX}=m_{XQ}\iff \frac{b}{a-1}=\frac{3n}{-2m-1}\iff
          n\cdot 3(1-a)=b(2m-1),\tag{ $\clubsuit$ }\]
which is true from  $(1)$ . 
