Let  $I$  be the incentre of  $\triangle ABC$ . We prove that  $H$ ,  $O$  and  $I$  are collinear. **<span style="color:#0B5394">Lemma.</span>** The lines  $FI$ ,  $DE$  and  $CM$  are concurrent at a point  $X$ .

*Proof.*  Let  $\{X\}=FI\cap DE$ , let  $K$  and  $L$  be the points where the line parallel to  $AB$  passing through  $X$  meets sides  $AC$  and  $BC$ , respectively. Then the projections of  $I$  onto the sides of  $\triangle CKL$  are  $E$ ,  $X$  and  $D$ , which are collinear so by the reverse of Simson's theorem it follows that  $I\in (CKL)$ . Because  $CI$  bisects  $\angle C$  it follows that  $IK=IL$ . But  $IX\perp AB\parallel KL$ , so  $X$  is the midpoint of the segment  $KL$ . But this means that  $M=h(X)$  where  $h$  is the homothety centered at  $C$  which maps  $KL\mapsto AB$ , so  $X\in CM$ .  $\blacksquare$ Now let  $V$  and  $W$  be the midpoints of the arcs  $\widehat{AB}$  and  $\widehat{ACB}$ , respectively, and  $P$  be the midpoint of segment  $DE$ ,  $\{Q\}=MM'\cap DE$ .

<span style="color:#0B5394">**Lemma.**</span> The points  $I$ ,  $Q$  and  $W$  are collinear.

*Proof.* Let  $\{Q'\}=WI\cap DE$ . By  $CE=DE$ ,  $WA=WB$  and  $\angle AWB=\angle C$  it follows that  $\triangle BWA\sim\triangle DCE$ . Hence if  $f$  is the spiral similarity which maps  $DE\mapsto BA$  then  $f(C)=W$ , and also  $f(P)=M$ ,  $f(I)=V$ , which implies 
\[\frac{VM}{WM}=\frac{IP}{CP}=\frac{IQ'}{Q'W}\]
the latter equality being true by Thales' theorem in  $\triangle ICW$  (since  $CW\perp CI\perp DE$  implies  $CW\parallel PQ$ ). Therefore, by the reverse Thales' theorem in  $\triangle WIU$  it follows that  $MQ'\parallel VI\perp DE$ , so  $Q=Q'$ .  $\blacksquare$  
