[/asy]**<span style="color:#0B5394">Claim 1.</span>**  $DY=2DN\sin B$ .

*Proof.* Let  $NE\cap (BDE)=X'$  and  $NF\cap (CDF)=Y'$ . Since  $\angle BEC=90^\circ$  we get  $NE=NB$ , so  $\angle NEB=\angle NBE$  so  $\angle
X'EB=\angle DBE$ , and because  $BDX'E$  cyclic we get that it is an isosceles trapezoid. Hence  $DX'\parallel BE$  so  $NX'=ND$  and also  $\angle NX'D =\angle CBE=90^\circ-C$ . Similarly  $NY' =ND$  and  $\angle NY'D=90^\circ-B$ . hence  $N$  circumcentre of  $DX'Y'$  and so \[\angle
DX'Y'=\frac12\angle DNY'=\frac12(180^\circ-2\angle NY'D)=\frac12(180^\circ-2(90^\circ-B))=B.\] Similarly  $\angle DY'X'=C$ . Since  $\angle KX'D=180^\circ-B$  because  $BDX'K$  cyclic we get  $\angle KX'Y'=180^\circ$  so  $K\in X'Y'$ . Similarly  $L\in X'Y'$ , so  $K, L, X', Y'$  collinear.
Since  $X'\in (BDE), Y'\in (CDF)$  we get  $X'=X$  and  $Y'=Y$ ,  $\triangle DXY\sim\triangle ABC$ ,  $N$  circumcentre of  $DX$  and  $N\in EX\cap FY$ . 
These easily imply  $DY=2DN\sin B$ .  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:#0B5394">Claim 2.</span>**  $DH/DY=\sin \gamma/\sin (\beta-\gamma)$ .

*Proof.* Since  $BDH$  isosceles with vertex  $B$  we get  $DH=2\sin \beta BD=2(\sin\beta)(s-b)$ , and since \[DY=2DN\sin B=2(a/2-s+b)\sin B=(b-c)\sin B\] we get that the claim is equivalent to
\begin{align*}
\frac{2(s-b)\sin\beta}{(b-c)\sin B}=\frac{\sin \gamma}{\sin
    (\beta-\gamma)}& \Leftrightarrow \frac{(s-b)\sin\beta}{(b-c)\sin \beta\cos
    \beta}=\frac{\sin \gamma}{\sin
                     \beta\cos\gamma-\sin\gamma\cos\beta}
  &\Leftrightarrow (s-b)\sin\beta\cos\gamma-(s-b)\sin\gamma\cos\beta=(b-c)\sin\gamma \cos\beta 
&\Leftrightarrow (s-b)\sin\beta\cos\gamma=(s-b+b-c)\sin\gamma\cos\beta
                   & \Leftrightarrow (s-b)\tan\beta=(s-c)\tan\gamma
\end{align*}
which is true since both sides equal  $r$ .  $\blacksquare$ But by the Law of Sines  $\dfrac{DH}{DY}=\dfrac{\sin \angle DYH}{\sin\angle DHY}$ , so  $\dfrac{\sin \angle DYH}{\sin\angle DHY}=\dfrac{\sin \gamma}{\sin (\beta-\gamma)}$ . Since \[\angle DYH+\angle DHY=180^\circ -(\angle HDC+\angle YDN)=180^\circ-((180^\circ-(90^\circ \beta))+90^\circ-B)=\gamma+(\beta-\gamma)\] and by the monotonicity of  $\sin$  it follows that  $\angle DYH=\gamma$ , so since  $\triangle DXY\sim\triangle ABC$  it follows that  $YH$  is the  $Y$ -angle bisector in  $DXY$ . Similarly  $XG$  is the  $X$ -angle bisector, so  $J=XG\cap YH$ . But since  $\triangle DXY\sim\triangle ABC$  we get
\begin{align*}
\angle GJD+\angle GHD&=\angle
                       XJD+(180^\circ-(90^\circ-A/2)-(90^\circ-B/2))
  &=(90^\circ+C/2) +
    (A/2+B/2)=90^\circ+(A+B+C)/2=2\cdot90^\circ=180^\circ
\end{align*}
so  $GJDH$  is a cyclic quadrilateral, meaning  $J$  lies on the incircle of  $ABC$ , as desired.

