*Proof.* This is actually quite well-known, but for those who didn't read the handout Muricaaaa, let  $SM\cap (ABC)\setminus  S=X$ ,  $A'D\cap (ABC)\setminus A'=X'$ . It is well-known that  $H\in SM$ , so  $\angle AXH=90^\circ$ , which implies that  $AX$  is the radical axis of  $(AEF)$  and  $(ABC)$ . Therefore  $AX$ ,  $EF$  and  $BC$  concur at the radical centre  $Y$  of  $(AEF), (ABC)$  and  $(BCEF)$ . But as  $AD, BE, CF$  concur at  $H$ , by Ceva-Menelaus it follows that  $-1=(Y, D; B, C)\stackrel{A}{=}(X, H_1; B, C).$  On the other hand, as  $AA'$  symmedian,  $ABA'C$  is a harmonic quadrilateral, so  $-1=(A, A'; B, C)\stackrel{D}{=} (H_1, X'; C, B),$  which means  $(X', H_1;B, C)=-1=(X, H_1; B, C)$ , so  $X=X'$ .  $\blacksquare$ [asy]
