Let  $O$  the the centre of  $(ABC)$ . Denote the proposition " $P\notin\{A,B,C\}$  and the perpendiculars from  $A_P$  to  $BC$ , from  $B_P$  to  $CA$ , and from  $C_P$  to  $AB$  are concurrent" by  $\mathcal P$ .**<span style="color:#0B5394">Claim.</span>**  $\mathcal P$  is equivalent to  $\angle (OP, AB)=\angle (OP, CP)$ ,  $\angle (OP, BC)=\angle (OP, AP)$  and  $\angle (OP, CA)=\angle (OP, BP)$ .

*Proof.* Let the parallel through  $O$  to  $BC$  intersect line  $AP$  and  $A'$ , and similarly we define  $B'$  and  $C'$ , respectively. Note that  $\angle C'OB'=\angle B'PC'\in \{A, 180^\circ-A\}$ , and similarly  $\angle C'OA'=\angle A'PC'$  and  $\angle B'OA'=\angle A'PB'$ .  $(1)$ Now, note that  $\angle (OP, AB)=\angle (OP, CP)$ ,  $\angle (OP, BC)=\angle (OP, AP)$  and  $\angle (OP, CA)=\angle (OP, BP)$  is equivalent to 
\[C'O=C'P,\; A'O=A'P\text{ and } B'O=B'P\tag{2}\]
Note that if  $A', B', C'$  are collinear then by the Law of Sines
\[\frac{A'B'}{A'C'}=\frac{\sin \angle A'OB'\cdot OB'/\sin\angle OA'B'}{\sin\angle A'OC'\cdot OC'/\sin\angle OA'C'}=\frac{OB'}{OC'}\frac{\sin\angle A'OB'}{\sin\angle A'OC'}\stackrel{(1)}{=}\frac{OB'}{OC'}\frac{\sin\angle B'PA'}{\sin\angle C'PA'},\]
and similarly by the Law of Sines we get  $\frac{A'B'}{A'C'}=\frac{PB'}{PC'}\frac{\sin\angle B'PA'}{\sin\angle C'PA'}$ , which implies  $\frac{OB'}{OC'}=\frac{PB'}{PC'}$ . From this and  $(1)$  it follows that  $\triangle B'OC'\sim \triangle B'PC'$ , and as  $B'C'=B'C'$  this means  $\triangle B'OC'\cong \triangle B'PC'$ . Therefore  $B'C'$  is the perpendicular bisector of segment  $OP$ , which implies  $(2)$ . As  $(2)$  clearly implies that  $A', B'$  and  $C'$  are collinear on the perpendicular bisector of  $OP$ , it follows that  $A', B'$  and  $C'$  are collinear iff  $(2)$  holds true.
Hence we've reduced the problem to showing that  $\mathcal P$  is equivalent to the collinearity of  $A'$ ,  $B'$  and  $C'$ . But this equivalence is true because the perpendiculars from  $A_P$  to  $BC$ , from  $B_P$  to  $CA$ , and from  $C_P$  to  $AB$  are simply the polars of  $A'$ ,  $B'$  and  $C'$  wrt  $(ABC)$ . Indeed, as  $A'\in PA$ , by La Hire's theorem it follows that  the polar  $p$  of  $A'$  wrt  $(ABC)$  passes through the pole of  $PA$  wrt  $(ABC)$ , which is  $A_P$ , and moreover  $p\perp OA'\parallel BC$ .  $\blacksquare$ [asy]
import olympiad;
size(11cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(12pt));
pair A = dir(125);
pair B = dir(200);
pair P = dir(221.5);
pair C = dir(340); draw(A--B--C--cycle, black+1);
pair O = circumcenter(A, B, C);
pair Q = 2*foot(C, P, O)-C;
pair Ap = 2/conj(A+P);
pair Bp = 2/conj(B+P);
pair Cp = 2/conj(C+P);
pair Ao = B-C; pair Ad=extension(O, Ao, A, P);
pair Bo = C-A; pair Bd=extension(O, Bo, B, P);
pair Co = A-B; pair Cd=extension(O, Co, C, P);
pair Af = foot(Ap, B, C); pair Bf = foot(Bp, A, C);
pair X = extension(Ap, Af, Bp, Bf); 
draw(Bd--Cd, dashed);
draw(Ap--Cp); draw(A--Ap); draw(B--Bp); draw(C--Cp);
draw(circumcircle(A,B,C));
draw(O--P, blue);
draw(P--A); draw(P--B); draw(P--C);
draw(Cd--O--Bd--P--Ad--O);
draw(Ap--X--Bp, dashed); draw(X--Cp, dashed);
draw(P--Q);
dot(" $A$ ",A,dir(120));
dot(" $B$ ",B,dir(45));
dot(" $C$ ",C,dir(350));
dot(" $O$ ",O,dir(50));
dot(" $P$ ",P,dir(240));
dot(" $A_P$ ",Ap,dir(220));
dot(" $B_P$ ",Bp,dir(180));
dot(" $C_P$ ",Cp,dir(-10));
dot(" $A'$ ",Ad,dir(220));
dot(" $B'$ ",Bd,dir(220));
dot(" $C'$ ",Cd,dir(-90));
dot(" $Q$ ",Q,dir(90));
dot(X);
[/asy]
Now, to finish the problem, we work with the complex plane where  $(ABC)$  is the unit circle and the affixes of points are denoted by lowercase letters. If we denote  $\angle (OP, AB)=\angle (OP, CP)$  by  $(*)$ , it's clear that  $(*)$  is equivalent to  $PO$  bisecting  $\angle QPC$  where  $Q\in (ABC)$  with  $PQ\parallel AB$ , which is equivalent with 
\begin{align*}
\frac{q+c}p\in\mathbb R&\Leftrightarrow \frac{ab/p+c}p= \frac{ab}{p^2}+\frac{c}p\in\mathbb R\Leftrightarrow \frac{ab}{p^2}+\frac{c}p=\overline{\left(\frac{ab}{p^2}\right)}+\overline{\left(\frac{c}p\right)}
&\Leftrightarrow \frac{ab}{p^2}+\frac{c}{p}=\frac{p^2}{ab}+\frac{p}c\Leftrightarrow a^2b^2c+abc^2p=p^4c+abp^3
&\Leftrightarrow 0=a^2b^2c+abc^2p-p^4c-abp^3=(ab+cp)(abc-p^3).
\end{align*}
Using analogous computation and our claim above we get that  $\mathcal P$  is equivalent with 
\[0=(ab+cp)(abc-p^3)=(bc+ap)(abc-p^3)=(ca+bp)(abc-p^3),\]
which is equivalent with  $p^3=abc$ . As the roots of  $x^3=abc$  are the affixes of three points on the unit circle which are the vertices of an equilateral triangle, the problem is solved.**Remark.** By replacing our claim with the claim that  $\mathcal P$  holds if and only if the  $P$ -Simson line of  $ABC$  passes through its nine-point centre, and then using a known property on perpendicular Simson lines combined with some Romanian IMO 2009 TST problem, we get another, more complicated, solution, which can be extended to show that our equilateral triangle is (inversely) homothetic to the inner Morley triangle fo  $ABC$ .

