Suppose that for some integers  $a$  and  $b$ , the number  $bp-aq$  is not the difference between two positions of the frog. We will show that  $bp-aq \geq p+q$ .

A sequence of frog hops corresponds to an up-right path  $\mathcal P$  where U corresponds to  $p$ -hops and R corresponds to  $q$ -hops. WLOG  $\mathcal P$  begins from  $(0,0)$  and ends at  $(kp,kq)$  for some positive integer  $k$ . Consider the lattice  $L$  spanned by the vectors  $\langle a, b \rangle$  and  $\langle kp, kq \rangle$ ; for each point  $\overrightarrow{v}$  in  $L$ , draw a copy of  $\mathcal P$  that goes from  $\overrightarrow{v}$  to  $\overrightarrow{v}+\langle kp, kq \rangle$ .**Claim:** No two copys of  $\mathcal P$  intersect except at endpoints.**Proof:** It suffices to check that  $\mathcal P$  does not intersect  $\mathcal P + \langle a, b \rangle$ . Indeed, if they intersected at a point  $\overrightarrow{u}$ , then both  $\overrightarrow{u} - \langle a, b \rangle$  and  $\overrightarrow{u}$  would lie on  $\mathcal P$ ; these two points correspond to points in the frog's journey that are  $bp-aq$  apart, contradiction.**Claim:** The determinant of  $L$  is at least  $kp+kq$ .**Proof:** Consider the  $kp+kq$  lattice points on  $\mathcal P$  excluding  $(kp, kq)$ . For each such point, map it to the square that it is the top-left corner of. If we call these  $kp+kq$  squares the *territory* of  $\mathcal P$ , it is easy to see that any two copies of  $\mathcal P$  have disjoint territories. The determinant must be at least the size of the territory, proving the claim.

So,  $\det \begin{pmatrix} a  & kp   b & kq \end{pmatrix} \geq kp+kq$ , which is equivalent to what we wanted to show.

[asy]
import graph;

// Euclidean length of a vector
real vlen(pair v) {
  return sqrt(v.x^2 + v.y^2);
}

// Draw gridlines in the given window
void drawGrid(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax) {
  pen gridpen = gray(0.8) + 0.3bp;
  for (int x = ceil(xmin); x <= floor(xmax); ++x)
    draw((x,ymin)--(x,ymax), gridpen);
  for (int y = ceil(ymin); y <= floor(ymax); ++y)
    draw((xmin,y)--(xmax,y), gridpen);
}

// Main routine
void tilePath(path P, pair v1, pair v2,
              real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax) {

  // Draw grid first
  drawGrid(xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax);

  // Bounding box outline (optional)
  draw(box((xmin,ymin),(xmax,ymax)), gray(0.4));

  // Estimate how many lattice steps we need in each direction
  real len1 = vlen(v1);
  real len2 = vlen(v2);
  real minlen = min(len1, len2);
  if (minlen == 0) minlen = max(len1, len2); // handle degenerate case
  if (minlen == 0) minlen = 1;               // worst case fallback

  pair diag = (xmax - xmin, ymax - ymin);
  real radius = vlen(diag) / minlen + 3;
  int R = ceil(radius);

  // Loop over lattice points i*v1 + j*v2
  for (int i = -R; i <= R; ++i) {
    for (int j = -R; j <= R; ++j) {

      pair v = i*v1 + j*v2;

      // We only care about paths that might hit the window.
      // P goes from (0,0) to v2 = (p,q) with p,q > 0.
      // The translated copy goes from v to v+v2.
      // Quick intersection test with the rectangle:
      if (v.x <= xmax && v.x + v2.x >= xmin &&
          v.y <= ymax && v.y + v2.y >= ymin) {

        path Q = shift(v)*P;
        draw(Q, orange + 1bp);
      }
    }
  }
  for (int i = -R; i <= R; ++i) {
    for (int j = -R; j <= R; ++j) {

      pair v = i*v1 + j*v2;

      // Draw lattice point if inside window
      if (xmin <= v.x && v.x <= xmax &&
          ymin <= v.y && v.y <= ymax) {
        dot(v, royalblue+4);
      }
    }
  }

  // Clip picture to the window so partially visible paths
  // are cut off cleanly at the boundary
  clip(currentpicture, box((xmin,ymin),(xmax,ymax)));
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------
// Example usage
// -----------------------------------------------------------

unitsize(0.4cm);

// Up right path P from (0,0) to (p,q) = (3,2)
path P = (0,0)
         -- (1,0)
         -- (1,1)
         -- (2,1)
         -- (3,1)
         -- (3,2)
         -- (4,2)
         -- (4,3);

// Lattice vectors
pair v1 = (1,-2);   // (a,b)
pair v2 = (4,3);   // (p,q), must match endpoint of P

// Window
real xmin = -6, xmax = 10;
real ymin = -5, ymax = 7;

fill((0,0)--(0,-1)--(2,-1)--(2,0)--(4,0)--(4,1)--(5,1)--(5,2)--(3,2)--(3,1)--(2,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle, RGB(245,219,171));
// Draw everything
tilePath(P, v1, v2, xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax);
draw((xmin,ymin)--(xmin,ymax)--(xmax,ymax)--(xmax,ymin)--cycle);

draw(P, orange+2bp);
dot((0,0), royalblue+5.5);
dot((4,3), royalblue+4);
[/asy]

