solution_md:
We proceed by complex bashing. Let  $\triangle ABC$  be inscribed in the unit circle, and let  $\lambda = \frac{BM}{BC} = \frac{CN}{BC}$ , so that
\begin{align*}
|a|=|b|=|c|&=1 
o &= 0 
e &= \frac12\left(a+b+c-\frac{ac}b\right) 
f &= \frac12\left(a+b+c-\frac{ab}c\right) 
m &= \lambda c + (1-\lambda)b 
n &= \lambda b + (1-\lambda)c 
\end{align*}
Now let  $U$  be the circumcenter of  $\triangle MBF$ , and let  $X$  be the second intersection of  $\overleftrightarrow{BE}$  with the unit circle, so that  $$ x = -\frac{ac}b $$  We find the coordinate of  $U$  by shifting by  $b$ . Let  $u' = u-b$ , and let
\begin{align*}
m' = m-b &= \lambda(c-b) 
f' = f-b &= \frac{(c+a)(c-b)}{2c}
\end{align*}
so that  $\overline{m'} = -\frac{m'}{bc}$  and  $\overline{f'} = -\frac{f'}{ab}$ . Then  $$ u' = \frac{m'f'(\overline{m'}-\overline{f'})}{\overline{m'}f'-m'\overline{f'}} = \frac{am'-cf'}{a-c} = \frac{\frac{(b-c)(a+c)}2 - \lambda a(b-c)}{a-c} = \frac{(b-c)\left[c + (1-2\lambda)a\right]}{2(a-c)} $$  and
\begin{align*}
p &= 2\left[\frac12\left(b+x+u-bx\overline{u}\right)\right] - b 
&= x+u-bx\overline{u} 
&= b+u'-bx\overline{u'} 
&= b+u'+ac\overline{u'} 
&= b + \frac{(b-c)\left[c+(1-2\lambda)a\right]}{2(a-c)} + \frac{a(b-c)\left[a+(1-2\lambda)c\right]}{2b(a-c)} 
&= \frac{a^2b-a^2c+(3-2\lambda)ab^2-(1-2\lambda)ac^2-b^2c-bc^2}{2b(a-c)}
\end{align*}
Then we find the vector
\begin{align*}
p-f &= \frac{a^2b-a^2c+(3-2\lambda)ab^2-(1-2\lambda)ac^2-b^2c-bc^2}{2b(a-c)} - \frac{ac+bc+c^2-ab}{2c} 
&= \frac{a^2b^2-a^2c^2+(1-2\lambda)ab^2c-(1-2\lambda)ac^3}{2bc(a-c)} 
&= \frac{a(b-c)(b+c)\left[a + (1-2\lambda)c\right]}{2bc(a-c)}
\end{align*}
and so we have the ratio  $$ \frac{p-f}{\overline{p}-\overline{f}} = \frac{a^2\left[a+(1-2\lambda)c\right]}{c+(1-2\lambda)a} $$  Now we find the coordinate of the intersection  $T$  of  $\overleftrightarrow{PF}$  and  $\overleftrightarrow{AO}$ . It will suffice to show that the coordinate of  $T$  is symmetric under the map  $b\leftrightarrow c$ , since this map switches  $m\leftrightarrow n$ ,  $e\leftrightarrow f$ , and  $p\leftrightarrow q$ , so it will follow then that  $T$  lies on  $\overleftrightarrow{QE}$  as well. Now since  $T$  lies on  $\overleftrightarrow{AO}$ , we have  $\overline{t} = \frac{t}{a^2}$ . And since  $T$  lies on  $\overleftrightarrow{PF}$ , we have  $$ \frac{t-f}{\overline{t}-\overline{f}} = \frac{p-f}{\overline{p}-\overline{f}} $$    $$ \frac{t-\frac{ac+bc+c^2-ab}{2c}}{\frac{t}{a^2}-\frac{ab+ac+bc-c^2}{2abc}} = \frac{a^2\left[a+(1-2\lambda)c\right]}{c+(1-2\lambda)a} $$    $$ \frac{a^2b(2ct-ac-bc-c^2+ab)}{2bct-a^2b-a^2c-abc+ac^2} = \frac{a^2(a+\gamma c)}{c+\gamma a} $$  (where  $\gamma = 1-2\lambda$ )  $$ b(\gamma a+c)(2ct-ac-bc-c^2+ab) = (a+\gamma c)(2bct-a^2b-a^2c-abc+ac^2) $$  Then
\begin{align*}
t &= \frac{b(\gamma a+c)(ac+bc+c^2-ab) - a(a+\gamma c)(ab+ac+bc-c^2)}{2bc(\gamma a+c) - 2bc(a+\gamma c)} 
&= \frac{\gamma a(b^2c-ab^2-ac^2+c^3) + (abc^2+b^2c^2+bc^3-ab^2c-a^3b-a^3c-a^2bc+a^2c^2)}{2bc(\gamma-1)(a-c)} 
&= -\frac{2\lambda a(a-c)(b^2+c^2) + (abc^2+b^2c^2+bc^3-a^2b^2-a^3b-a^3c-a^2bc+ac^3)}{4\lambda bc(a-c)} 
&= -\frac{2\lambda a(a-c)(b^2+c^2) - (b+c)(a+b)(a+c)(a-c)}{4\lambda bc(a-c)} 
&= \frac{(a+b)(a+c)(b+c) - 2\lambda a(b^2+c^2)}{4\lambda bc}
\end{align*}
This is symmetric in  $b,c$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$

