solution_md:
The answer is  $\boxed{(m-1)(n-1)+1}$ . For convenience, let  $\mathcal{P}$  and  $\mathcal{Q}$  be each polygons. A crucial condition here is that  $\mathcal{P}$  and  $\mathcal{Q}$  are both connected, which means for any two cells in the same polygon, there is a path from one to the other, where two cells are connected iff they are adjacent by sides. For each dissect Bob can make, we call a domino  $bad$  when its two cells are each black and white. (this means that one cell is in  $\mathcal P$  and the other is in  $\mathcal Q$ )

<span style="color:#00f">**Observation. (Folklore)**</span> If we apply usual chessboard coloring(with two colors red and blue), it can be easily shown that the difference of red cells and blue cells in  $\mathcal{P}$ , is the minimum number of bad dominoes. (Note that the difference is the same for  $\mathcal{P}$  and  $\mathcal{Q}$ ) The proof of this is quite straightforward, so it is left for the readers. For example, the following polygon in the diagram needs at least  $3$  bad dominoes as there are  $5$  blue cells and  $2$  red cells. 
[asy]
int m = 6, n = 4;

for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
  for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    pair bl = (i, j); // bottom-left of the square
    pair tr = (i + 1, j + 1); // top-right

    if ((i + j) % 2 == 0)
      fill(bl--(i+1,j)--tr--(i,j+1)--cycle, red+white);
    else
      fill(bl--(i+1,j)--tr--(i,j+1)--cycle, paleblue);

    draw(bl--(i+1,j)--tr--(i,j+1)--cycle);
  }
draw((1,3)--(1,2)--(2,2)--(2,1)--(3,1)--(3,2)--(4,2)--(4,1)--(5,1)--(5,2)--(6,2)--(6,3)--cycle, black + linewidth(3));
}
[/asy]
-----
<span style="color:#00f">**Claim 1.**</span> For any  $2 \times 2$  box, it is impossible that both red cells are in  $\mathcal P$  and both blue cells are in  $\mathcal Q$ .
<span style="color:#f00">**Proof.**</span> 
[asy]
int m=7,n=5;
for(int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
for(int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
if(i < m-1) draw((i-0.5, j-0.5)--(i + 1 - 0.5, j-0.5));
if(j < n-1)draw((i-0.5, j-0.5)--(i-0.5, j + 1 - 0.5));
}
}
draw((1,2)--(1,3)--(4,3)--(4,1)--(2,1),red);
draw((2,2)--(5,2)--(5,0)--(1,0)--(1,1),blue);
fill((0.5, 1.5)--(1.5, 1.5)--(1.5, 2.5)--(0.5, 2.5)--cycle, red+white);
fill((1.5, 0.5)--(1.5, 1.5)--(2.5, 1.5)--(2.5, 0.5)--cycle, red+white);
fill((0.5, 0.5)--(1.5, 0.5)--(1.5, 1.5)--(0.5, 1.5)--cycle, paleblue);
fill((1.5, 1.5)--(2.5, 1.5)--(2.5, 2.5)--(1.5, 2.5)--cycle, paleblue);
label(" $b_2$ ",(1,1));
label(" $r_1$ ", (1,2));
label(" $r_2$ ", (2,1));
label(" $b_1$ ", (2,2));
dot((4,2), black);
[/asy]
Assume that both red cells  $r_1, r_2$  are in  $\mathcal P$  and both blue cells  $b_1, b_2$  are in  $\mathcal Q$ . Since  $\mathcal P$  and  $\mathcal Q$  are connected, there is a path  $p_r$  connecting  $r_1$  and  $r_2$  only passing through the cells in  $\mathcal P$ . Since this path makes a closed area, exactly one of  $b_i$  (  $i = 1, 2$  ) is inside the area. WLOG let that  $b_1$  was the cell. If we consider a path  $p_b$  connecting  $b_1$  and  $b_2$  only passing through cells in  $\mathcal Q$ , there must be an intersection between  $p_r$  and  $p_b$  as  $b_1$  is inside a closed area  $p_r$  makes and  $b_2$  does not. Thus as the intersection is both in  $\mathcal P$  and  $\mathcal Q$ , it is absurd. 
-----
<span style="color:#00f">**Claim 2.**</span> If we let  $X$  be the set of cells on the boundary of  $2m \times 2n$  board, the difference of red cells and blue cells in  $\mathcal P \cap X$  is at most  $1$ . 
<span style="color:#f00">**Proof.**</span> If  $|\mathcal P \cap X| \le 1$ , the result is obvious so we only consider the case when  $|\mathcal P \cap X| \ge 2$ . Let  $u ,v$  be two cells in  $\mathcal P \cap X$ . Say that  $X - \{u, v\}$  is partitioned into two connected parts  $X_1$  and  $X_2$ . Since there is a path  $p$  connecting  $u$  and  $v$  only passing through  $\mathcal P$ , if both  $|X_j \cap \mathcal Q| \ge 1$ , we can say that the path connecting two cells each in  $X_j \cap \mathcal Q$ , and  $p$  intersects. This intersection cell is both in  $\mathcal P$  and  $\mathcal Q$ , which is a contradiction, implying that at least one of  $X_j \cap \mathcal Q$  is empty. From this, we can deduce that  $X$  is partitioned into two connected set of cells  $\mathcal P \cap X$  and  $\mathcal Q \cap X$ . Let  $\mathcal P \cap X = \{ c_1, c_2, \cdots, c_k \}$ . If  $c_i$  is red,  $c_{i+1}$  is blue as its adjacent to  $c_i$ , and the opposite goes the same. Therefore, the difference of red cells and blue cells in  $\mathcal P \cap X$  is at most  $1$ . ( Note that the equality holds when  $2 \nmid k$  )

[asy]
int m = 8, n = 6;

for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
  for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    pair bl = (i, j); // bottom-left
    pair tr = (i + 1, j + 1); // top-right

    // Fill border squares with light gray
    if (i == 0 || i == m - 1 || j == 0 || j == n - 1) {
      fill(bl--(i, j + 1)--tr--(i + 1, j)--cycle, 0.8*white + 0.2*green);
    }

    // Draw the square border
    draw(bl--(i + 1, j)--tr--(i, j + 1)--cycle);
  }
}
draw((3.5,0.8)--(3.5,1.5)--(3.5,2.5)--(4.5,2.5)--(4.5, 3.5)--(5.5,3.5)--(5.5,4.5)--(5.5, 5.2), black);
label(" $u$ ", (3.5, 0.5));
label(" $v$ ", (5.5, 5.5));
dot((2.5, 5.5), black);
dot((7.5, 2.5), black);
draw((2.5, 5.5)--(7.5,2.5),dashed);
[/asy]

-----
From  $\mathcal P - X$  is in the interior of  $2m \times 2n$  board, which is the leftover cells after getting rid of  $X$ . Since it is a  $2(m-1) \times 2(n-1)$  board, we can partition it into  $(m-1) \times (n-1)$  number of  $2\times 2$  square boxes. From first claim, we can say that in each  $2 \times 2$  box, the difference of red cells and blue cells in  $\mathcal P$  is at most  $1$ . 
[asy]
int m = 8, n = 6;

for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
  for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
    pair bl = (i, j); // bottom-left of the square
    pair tr = (i + 1, j + 1); // top-right

    if ((i + j) % 2 == 0)
      fill(bl--(i+1,j)--tr--(i,j+1)--cycle, 0.1red + 0.9white);
    else
      fill(bl--(i+1,j)--tr--(i,j+1)--cycle, 0.3paleblue+0.7white);

    draw(bl--(i+1,j)--tr--(i,j+1)--cycle);
  }
draw((1,1)--(7,1)--(7,5)--(1,5)--cycle, black + linewidth(3));
draw((3,1)--(3,5), black + linewidth(3));
