Combining this with the second claim, the minimum number of bad dominoes used for  $\mathcal P \sqcup \mathcal Q$  is at most  $(m-1) \times (n-1) + 1$ . ( i.e, Bob can always dissect the whole grid where bad dominoes are less than or equal to  $(m-1)(n-1) + 1$  )Therefore, for any partition Alice can make, Bob can dissect the board into dominoes such that there are at most  $(m-1)(n-1) + 1$  number of bad dominoes. 

The optimal partition Alice can make, can be constructed easily by forcing the difference of red cells and blue cells in  $\mathcal P$  exactly  $(m-1)(n-1) + 1$ , which implies that we need at least  $(m-1)(n-1) + 1$  bad dominoes.

