\equiv a_{1} + a_{2} +...+ a_{x-1} + a_{x}$  mod  $9$ , with  $1 \leq a_{i} \leq 9$ , proving our lemma. $S(n)-S(n + a) \equiv 2018$  mod  $9$  $\Leftrightarrow n-n-a \equiv -a \equiv 2$  mod  $9$  $\Leftrightarrow a \equiv 7$  mod  $9$ The smallest positive integer possible for  $a \equiv 7$  mod  $9$  is  $a=7$ , so if  $a=7$  satisfy the conditions, it will be the answer.
Let  $n = 1\underbrace{000\cdots0}_{k} \underbrace{99\dots9}_{225}$ , for any  $1 \leq k$  with  $ k \in Z,$  i.e.  $n = 10^{k+225} + 10^{226} -1$ . $\Rightarrow n+a = n+7 = 1\underbrace{000\cdots0}_{k-1} 1\underbrace{000\dots0}_{224}6,$  i.e. $n = 10^{k+225} + 10^{256} + 6$  $S(n) = 1+ 225 \cdot9 = 226$  and  $S(n+a) = 8 \Rightarrow S(n) - S(n-a)= 2018$ . Once we have infinity many k that satisfies the condition  $ k \in Z,$  we have infinitely many n that satisfies the condition  $S(n)-S(n+7) = 2018 \Rightarrow a =7$

